1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,043
Last time, the job paid by itself,

2
00:00:03,493 --> 00:00:06,537
and the call afterward was just manners.

3
00:00:07,437 --> 00:00:11,785
Today the job isn't over until the crew reports in.

4
00:00:12,685 --> 00:00:14,424
And when they do,

5
00:00:14,874 --> 00:00:17,917
a second voice comes on the line.

6
00:00:19,717 --> 00:00:24,065
Along the way you'll learn which call sits on top,

7
00:00:24,515 --> 00:00:26,254
which ships get called,

8
00:00:26,704 --> 00:00:31,052
and what Comms can do with a call that's waiting.

9
00:00:32,252 --> 00:00:34,426
I start with the job.

10
00:00:35,326 --> 00:00:37,500
I take its reward off,

11
00:00:37,950 --> 00:00:42,298
and I point its Then line at a new key.

12
00:00:43,198 --> 00:00:47,546
Then I replace the beat below it with a quest.

13
00:00:49,346 --> 00:00:51,085
Look at what's different.

14
00:00:51,985 --> 00:00:58,072
There's no Beat line, so the crew will see it in the Quest Log.

15
00:00:58,972 --> 00:01:02,450
There's an objective, which sends them to Comms.

16
00:01:03,350 --> 00:01:05,524
The reward has moved here.

17
00:01:06,424 --> 00:01:10,337
The Action lines are the same two as before,

18
00:01:10,787 --> 00:01:13,396
because Action works on any quest.

19
00:01:15,196 --> 00:01:19,109
And look at what's missing: there's no Done when.

20
00:01:20,009 --> 00:01:23,922
Nothing in the game finishes this step by itself.

21
00:01:25,122 --> 00:01:27,296
So what does finish it?

22
00:01:27,746 --> 00:01:30,354
An answer from the crew does.

23
00:01:31,254 --> 00:01:34,298
Quill should be asking now, not congratulating,

24
00:01:34,748 --> 00:01:36,922
so I change her lines.

25
00:01:37,822 --> 00:01:41,300
And on the crew's answer, after the semicolon,

26
00:01:41,750 --> 00:01:44,793
I write completes, and the step's key.

27
00:01:46,593 --> 00:01:52,246
Now here's the rule for a call that a step is waiting on.

28
00:01:53,146 --> 00:01:57,928
Every answer that ends the call has to finish the step.

29
00:01:58,828 --> 00:02:02,741
If I add an answer that just hangs up,

30
00:02:03,191 --> 00:02:09,278
the call is gone, and the step stays in the Quest Log for good.

31
00:02:10,178 --> 00:02:12,352
And lint can't see that.

32
00:02:13,552 --> 00:02:18,335
A call can have more than one person on the line.

33
00:02:19,235 --> 00:02:23,583
Chief Ives has been in my cast since Lecture 2,

34
00:02:24,033 --> 00:02:25,772
with nothing to say.

35
00:02:26,672 --> 00:02:29,280
So I give him a block:

36
00:02:29,730 --> 00:02:34,513
an at sign, his key, and two takes of his own.

37
00:02:36,313 --> 00:02:38,922
The call still belongs to Quill,

38
00:02:39,372 --> 00:02:42,415
and it's her name on the list.

39
00:02:43,315 --> 00:02:45,489
But when Comms presses Continue,

40
00:02:45,939 --> 00:02:49,417
the name and the face change to his.

41
00:02:50,317 --> 00:02:56,404
Remember that the line is an at sign and a key, and nothing else.

42
00:02:57,304 --> 00:03:01,652
Write his full name there, and his block is gone,

43
00:03:02,102 --> 00:03:03,841
and lint says clean.

44
00:03:05,041 --> 00:03:10,259
Calls wait in a list, and the newest one is on top.

45
00:03:11,159 --> 00:03:16,811
So a call that matters can get pushed down by a later one.

46
00:03:17,711 --> 00:03:19,885
A call never times out,

47
00:03:20,335 --> 00:03:23,813
and only one is open at a time.

48
00:03:25,613 --> 00:03:30,830
To keep a call on top, I give its scene a priority.

49
00:03:31,730 --> 00:03:35,209
A higher number sits above a lower one,

50
00:03:35,659 --> 00:03:39,572
and a call with no priority counts as zero.

51
00:03:40,472 --> 00:03:45,254
Use it for a call that a step is waiting on,

52
00:03:45,704 --> 00:03:47,878
and leave the others alone.

53
00:03:49,078 --> 00:03:52,122
I've never said which ship she calls,

54
00:03:52,572 --> 00:03:54,746
and I don't have to,

55
00:03:55,196 --> 00:03:57,804
because every player ship is called.

56
00:03:58,704 --> 00:04:02,617
With one ship, that's all there is to it.

57
00:04:04,417 --> 00:04:09,200
With two, each ship gets its own copy of the call.

58
00:04:10,100 --> 00:04:14,883
But the job is shared, so there's only one of it.

59
00:04:15,783 --> 00:04:19,696
One crew says yes, and it starts for everybody.

60
00:04:20,596 --> 00:04:25,813
And the first crew to report gets the side paid, just once.

61
00:04:27,613 --> 00:04:32,396
So write your lines for any ship that might hear them,

62
00:04:32,846 --> 00:04:37,628
and keep jobs like this for missions flown by one ship.

63
00:04:38,828 --> 00:04:41,437
This part has nothing to type.

64
00:04:42,337 --> 00:04:47,554
It's what the person at Comms can do with what you wrote.

65
00:04:48,454 --> 00:04:51,063
They can leave a call waiting,

66
00:04:51,513 --> 00:04:53,252
they can open it,

67
00:04:53,702 --> 00:04:55,876
and they can press Back.

68
00:04:56,776 --> 00:05:01,124
There's a dial that chooses where the conversation is drawn.

69
00:05:02,024 --> 00:05:04,633
And every finished call is kept,

70
00:05:05,083 --> 00:05:07,691
so it can be read again.

71
00:05:09,491 --> 00:05:13,839
So an answer is a decision the crew makes once.

72
00:05:14,739 --> 00:05:19,957
Everything else lets them put it off, or look back at it.

73
00:05:21,157 --> 00:05:24,200
Lint names ten mistakes in this lecture,

74
00:05:24,650 --> 00:05:26,824
and the page lists them.

75
00:05:27,724 --> 00:05:29,898
These two it can't see.

76
00:05:30,798 --> 00:05:35,580
If I take the completes off the answer, lint says clean,

77
00:05:36,030 --> 00:05:39,074
and the step can never be finished.

78
00:05:40,874 --> 00:05:46,091
And if I leave the Beat line in, lint says clean again.

79
00:05:46,991 --> 00:05:49,600
The step still works and pays,

80
00:05:50,050 --> 00:05:53,528
but the crew is never told to report.

81
00:05:54,428 --> 00:05:58,341
So check both of those with your own eyes.

82
00:05:59,541 --> 00:06:01,280
Let's play it through.

83
00:06:02,180 --> 00:06:06,528
I take the job, the way we did last time.

84
00:06:07,428 --> 00:06:10,472
Thirty seconds later the beacon is set,

85
00:06:10,922 --> 00:06:14,835
and there's my new step, telling me to report.

86
00:06:15,735 --> 00:06:17,474
And there's the call.

87
00:06:19,274 --> 00:06:21,013
It opens on Quill,

88
00:06:21,463 --> 00:06:24,941
and when I press Continue, that's Chief Ives.

89
00:06:25,841 --> 00:06:31,059
I press Back, and the call waits, and so does the step.

90
00:06:31,959 --> 00:06:36,741
I open it again, and this time I give the answer.

91
00:06:37,641 --> 00:06:41,989
The step is done, and now the job has paid.

92
00:06:43,189 --> 00:06:47,537
Now it's your turn: put more voices on your calls.

93
00:06:48,437 --> 00:06:51,915
Give Ives a block in the first call,

94
00:06:52,365 --> 00:06:56,278
and let Captain Sable listen in on the offer.

95
00:06:57,178 --> 00:07:02,396
Then break the report on purpose, and watch what lint doesn't say.

96
00:07:04,196 --> 00:07:08,543
Next time, a side that remembers what the crew did.
